ne and  white blocks to alternative exons or  excellent extensions  the spliced reeds you might see  in the region might look something like  this  and what leaf cutter does is to  construct this intron graph where nodes  correspond to  starts and ends and splice junctions  detected in the rna sequencing data  and the  uh the the core thing that leafcutter  does is to look for these connected  components i think probably all of you  for this audience know what a connected  component is  and then the quantification we use is to  ask how many spliced reads mapped to  each splice junction relative to the  other splice junction in the same  connected component  we have some statistical machinery based  on a dirichlet multinomial generalized  linear model to test for splicing  differences between different conditions  or different genotypes  so we and others have used leaf cutter  to detect thousands of what are called  splicing qtls or associations between  common genetic variation and splicing  outcomes this is a figure from the most  recent gtex paper who used leafgutter  for their main splicing analysis  and we and others have also shown that  splicing qtls are often  just as enriched or even sometimes more  enriched than eqtls in gus this for  example is for multiple sclerosis it  depends which trait you look at which  way this goes but the big picture is you  don't want to ignore sqtls when you're  looking at the effect of non-coding  variation  but how are these variants actually  affecting splicing so i took this figure  from  again the most recent gtex paper  and here they looked at which regions in  the gene in the genome splicing qtls and  eqtls are enriched in and as you might  expect eqtls are enriched in promoters  and splicing qtls are enriched in  actually at the splice donor and the  splice acceptor and in known splice  regions  and for eqtls you do see this weak  enrichment in transcription factored  binding sites but noticeably missing  from this figure is anything about  splice factors or rbps more broadly  and we that's surprising because  you know our understanding of how  splicing is regulated is that it's  through a combination  of cis acting rna sequences in the  precursor messenger rna itself and then  transacting rna binding proteins splice  factors so  this is a complicated picture but there  are these exotic splicing enhancers it's  fine splicing silencers and equivalent  intronic  elements as well  and there's been surprisingly little  work trying to look at whether there are  snips that affect rbp binding and that  might therefore be affecting splicing  downstream there is a notable exception  which is this paper from grace gels  group  which looked at a large collection of uh  what's called e-clip uh profiling of  different rbps and two different cell  types so e-clip is sort of the chipseq  of um  rbp binding  and by counting uh heterozygote snips in  these two cell lines by counting the  number of reads coming from each allele  they could perform a statistical test  asking whether there seemed to be a  genetic effect on  rbp binding affinity and they were able  to find a decent number of these snips  that do seem to affect rbp binding but  of course they're pretty limited because  they can only look at heterozygote snips  in these two cell lines  so  we thought maybe  a way to go further than this would be  to pull multiple cell lines from  different individuals  and we were inspired to do this by work  from hunter phrases group at stanford  you've done a similar approach for  looking at transcription factor  binding effects using chip seek and  they've also had follow-up work using  attack seek and looking at dna  methylation as well  and there's a couple of reasons that  this works out very nicely so one is  that if you're interested in common  genetic variation you actually don't  need to pull very many individuals to  capture a  large proportion of common variants so  this is based on just genotype data from  gtex but  and we're going to end up using nine uh  pulling nine individuals and you can see  that you actually get almost 95 percent  of common snips represented even at this  relatively small  seeming sample size of course  partly we're being helped here by the  fact that we happen to be a diploid  species and also this is just  considering uh snips at least five  percent minor allele frequency so this  isn't going to work for rare variants  the other really nice  aspect of this is that we sort of  naturally control for the technical  variation that's the bane of large-scale  sequencing experiments  especially for functional genomics  because all of the experiments is being  done together  so  the way this is going to work is we're  pooling uh induced pluripotent stem  cells uh provided to us by kristen  brennan's lab at yale  and then we do  rna immunoprecipitation and sequencing  or rip seek um this is a sort of  competitor method to eclipse we're doing  this mainly because my postdoc meghan  scherzer who uh is driving this project  had generated beautiful rip c data  during her phd  and so there's a protocol we were very  comfortable with we also think it  actually has some advantages as well in  terms of specificity so  we if you focus on the cyan tracks here  these two are our data and this bottom  row is from uh encode we see rough  agreement in where peaks are our peaks  are broader which in this setting can  actually be good for capturing more  snips  and potentially more specific so you see  less non-specific binding outside of  clear peak regions  once we have our rip seek data from our  pool then we can try to  assess whether there's allele specific  binding  one fun thing you can do with this sort  of experiment is from the sequencing  data itself you can get an estimate of  how much each individual is represented  in the actual pool and we aim for that  to be even across the different  individuals but it's impossible to  guarantee that in practice  so the way this works is  to fit the simple linear regression  where y here is going to be the allelic  ratio maybe i should call this a  proportion for snip i  and then  the x's here are going to be the dosage  for individual eye and sorry individual  j and snip i  coded as 0 for homozygous reference 0.5  for a heterozygote and one for the  homozygote  alternate  and  these betas once we fit this model will  end up corresponding to the proportion  that individual j contributed to the  pool  when we run this on the two biological  replicates we have we see very good  consistency regardless of which um  sequencing output we look at so here  there are three different  you know precipitations that we did so  for hrp a k h9pa1 and rbsox2 and  importantly also you do it we you would  you always do an input to control for  expression variation so this is  effectively just a total rna sequencing  run  i'm not going to talk about the igg this  is to control for non-specific binding  we have sort of a  internal control by accident here and  that this cell line actually wasn't  included in this biological replica  and so it should be at zero so this  gives you some sense of what the  estimation error is here um of course  you can really have negative proportions  of a cell line so we uh we have an  improved version now where we can strain  those faders to be positive and so that  that effect  goes away  so  the main challenge in analyzing this  data is that we want to distinguish  allele specific expression effects from  allele-specific binding of the rbp  the way we do this is in a bayesian  probabilistic model which i'll try to  briefly describe  so  from those estimated proportions those  betas and the genotype data that we have  for the lines we have what we think is a  pretty accurate estimate of the allelic  ratio  of the  for the two alleles for each net at the  dna level  and then we model the unobserved true  allelic ratio in the rna input  as being beta distributed with mean  equal to the elite ratio and the dna and  then some concentration parameter which  controls how different they're going to  be globally and this is to model allele  specific expressions so is one allele  transcribed more than the other well it  could be effect on decay rate as well  the data that we have that's informative  about this olivic ratio are the allelic  counts in the rna input we model those  as being beta binomial distributed with  mean equal to this r  and a dispersion parameter d  then this is sort of the interesting bit  from the statistical statistical point  of view and the biology is whether there  are allele specific binding effects so  that would correspond to the true  allelic ratio in the ip'd data being  different  to the alien ratio in the input and  again we use the beta distribution here  with a mean equal to  the allele ratio of the rna level  um and another concentration parameter  that's going to be learned from the data  and then we uh the data that we have  that's informative about the allele  ratio in the ip are the allelic counts  for the ip and again we use a beta  binomial distribution here with another  dispersion counter  so  reasonably complicated probabilistic  model  but we were able to fit this very  efficiently  using pyro which is a probabilistic  programming language which sits on top  of pi torch and gives you all the  advantages that deep learning gets of  you know gpu-based computation and so on  we then have a statistical test uh based  on a z-score  and  for h9pk which is the rbp where we've  done the most the deepest sequencing  we estimate that over seven thousand  snips show allele specific binding  effects and that's about two percent of  testable snips  um for comparison the hunter phrases  hunter phrases were transcription  factors in a similar way  was also estimating between one and two  percent of testable snips having effects  on transcription factor binding um  depending on which specific  transcription factor you're looking at  this is the uh 10 fdl  of course what we're really interested  in doing with this is asking  whether these snips that affect rbp  binding seem to have downstream effect  on splicing and the way we're doing that  is through a statistical genetics  approach so we're taking  leafcutter splicing qtls from ipscs from  this nice meta-analysis  doing statistical fine mapping so this  gives you per variant posterior  inclusion probabilities so probability  that a specific snip is the actual  causal snip for a splicing change  and then we use a simple weighted  logistic regression which i can talk  about more people are interested  to estimate enrichment of these  different features so we have a couple  of control features whether the snip is  genic whether it's exotic whether it  falls in a binding peak for hrmpk  and then finally whether it's called as  a allele specific binding qtl  and all of these  get positive and significant  coefficients this is a regression model  so these effects are additive so a snip  that is in a binding peak  has is more likely to affect splicing  than otherwise but if it's also called  as a asb qtl then it has a considerably  greater chance of influencing splicing  so we're very pleased to see that um and  we're following up on this looking at  other um splice factors and um also  using more individuals as well  so the second vignette i want to talk  about is around causal network inference  so probably many of you are aware of  mendelian randomization since it's being  quite widely used in human genetics now  and the basic idea is to use snips as  what are called instrumental variables  to deal with this issue that if you're  trying to ask whether a specific  exposure x causally affects an outcome y  you're always worried about confounders  u so in mr  if you're willing to make the assumption  that your snips  do not  affect the confounder and only affect  the outcomes  via the exposure  then you can get an estimate of this uh  beta the causal effect of x on y  and you can even relax this assumption a  little bit that there's no other parts  from the snip to the outcome when you  have in the situation where you have  multiple snips and so the most widely  used approach is called ego regression  basically you plot  on the x-axis the association between  the instrument and the exposure the  y-axis the association between the  instrument and the outcome and the slope  of this ends up being a robust estimate  from theta  but in reality we don't normally just  have two traits that we're interested in  exposure and outcome in reality  phenotypes and traits live in complex  directed networks where there are  biomarkers and diseases machine  expression and also unmeasured  confounders so how do we think about  trying to do  media randomization in this in this more  sort of realistic setting  so we came up with  a relatively simple linear model which  we think captures a lot of what you'd  want to capture in these networks so  here  this is phenotype j  is given by  effects from other phenotypes through  this graph g  and then by genetic effects so x here  would be genotype and  beta being regression coefficients  finally gamma would represent unmeasured  confounding  and we can change this into matrix  notation shown here  and what we're really interested in  doing is asking whether we can separate  out direct effects of one trait on  another versus mediated effects through  that network  and in the case where you have  the this r matrix the total effect  matrix so this is  the true underlying effect of a on b for  every pair of traits a and b including  direct effects that are mediated through  the network  and then under the model i just showed  you it turns out you can  calculate exactly analytically g this  matrix of direct effects using this  expression  where here d is just a  term things worried about t measures is  a normalization factor the interesting  part really here is this inverse this  matrix inverse so if you've seen um  gaussian graphical models the graphical  lasso before this might look a little  familiar this is very analogous to  converting um correlations to partial  correlations where you're conditioning  on other  variables in your  network there are two problems with this  though the first is  even in the best case scenario not all  traits are going to be observed so we  need to worry about uh what that's  called correlated player trophy so those  are  confounders that have themselves have a  genetic component  and we i'm not going to go into it in  any detail but we recently published a  paper with a very straightforward  extension to egg regression that copes  with this there are also some elegant um  mixture model based approaches to  solving this problem um that are more  computationally intensive but work  better in some scenarios although also  worse than others  the second problem is we don't know  we can't even  we don't know the exact total causal  effect matrix we just have an estimate  of it r hat and unfortunately this is  not one of these situations where you  can just plug in an estimator and have  everything work out nicely and the  reason for that is this matrix inverse  is not very stable so you can imagine  this can if there's a lot of noise  contributing to small eigenvalues or  singular values in here then those are  going to get amplified by the matrix  inverse  the solution we came up with was to use  this convex optimization formulation of  this problem  so  here r is our total effect matrix or  estimate of it for mendelian  randomization  v is going to be an approximate matrix  inverse of r  and  that is  specified via this loss and then we have  this regularization which so this is a  lasso or graphical lasso like  regularization and it puts an l1 penalty  l1 penalty on every element of d  and we set this up in this slightly  convoluted way so by  constraining that matrix u times matrix  v has to be equal to the identity  because it lets us use this optimization  approach called alternating direction  method and multipliers which is a very  elegant approach for solving these sort  of biconvex optimization problems  we have these weights w which let us  account for standard errors coming from  the mr  we have a stability criterion adapted  from a similar approach to graphical  lasso for lambda  and the connection is graphical lasso  which gives you undirected networks so  there's a lot of why we're sort of um  you think this approach makes a lot of  sense  we've done a large number of i should  say brelin really has done a large  number of uh simulations generating  random graphs with different um  characteristics and also different ways  of orienting the edges  and we seem to be broadly robust to  the sort of network how the network  looks and  it's an interesting problem because  there aren't  very obvious baselines to compare to you  but the sort of simple baselines we can  come up with we perform much better than  the big uh sort of real world  application we've done with this is to  look at the uk biobank so we end up  looking at  just over 400 traits based on various  filters  out of those  around five percent have significant  total causal effects on each other  and those  8 000 or so total causal effects are  explained by a direct effect network  that we estimate that has just under 3  000 edges  and then we can start to look at  interesting properties of that directed  network so we can see that most nodes  have no causal effects  but there are  hub nodes that have a very large number  of downstream effects for example bmi  we also see very high connectivity so  any node  which influences or that affects at  least one other node can affect any node  in the network eventually  by propagating effects through the  network  we also see very pervasive mediation  so if you take a pair of nodes a pair of  traits that have a significant total  causal type between them the median path  length is actually two so that says it's  more common that when you see an effect  of a on b it's through an effect on c  rather than being direct and also the  shortest path between two nodes  on average only explains 52 percent of  bearing so there's if affects b there's  often two parts through the network in  which through which affects be  the direction we're going with this  project is to try to think about using  it to estimate the  directed  gene regulatory networks underlying  complex traits and to do this we need  large-scale trans eqtl and sqtl  mapping  to do that in collaboration with toffee  raj is lab at mount sinai we've been  collating and uniformly processing all  the  publicly available  post-mortem brain rna sequencing data we  can get our hands on  calling transient qtls and we think that  the sort of the sort of sample sizes  we're getting to around five or six  thousand individuals  we should start to see a significant  number of transient ttls and that does  seem to be to be playing out  this connects to work from my old lab at  stanford uh around the omnigenic  proposed homogenic model that says that  maybe one of the reasons many traits  look so polygenic is that gene  regulatory networks are so densely  connected that you can perturb almost  any gene in the network and that effect  will eventually propagate on to the  trait you're looking at  so  um acknowledgements here should mostly  go to megan and breeland he did the bulk  of this work um and i'm very happy to  take questions  thank you david it was all so clear  thank you very much for trying out  no problem  glad it's a good it's a one plus of the  pandemic is that uh  this stuff runs a lot more smoothly  these days because we're all used to  doing it

